\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 In the Ent/DagWood model, a 
\f2\fs26 TracePosition
\f0\fs24  is not just a number. It is a position on a specific branch of historical evolution.\
Ordering is a partial order over history.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 A <= B
\f0\fs24  means that 
\f2\fs26 A
\f0\fs24  is historically included in 
\f2\fs26 B
\f0\fs24 . Operationally, this means that if you start at 
\f2\fs26 B
\f0\fs24  and walk upward through branch-parent links, you eventually reach 
\f2\fs26 A
\f0\fs24 \'92s branch at a position at or beyond 
\f2\fs26 A.position
\f0\fs24 .\
This is not just tree ancestry, because the model already allows DAG-style merge structure through 
\f2\fs26 DagBranch
\f0\fs24 , which has two parents. So the ordering is best understood as:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls18\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 linear numeric order within a branch\
\ls18\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 historical reachability across branches\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 The navigation cache exists to make repeated comparisons against the same reference position efficient. For a fixed reference 
\f2\fs26 R
\f0\fs24 , it records:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls19\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 each reachable ancestor branch\
\ls19\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 the greatest position on that branch historically included in 
\f2\fs26 R
\f0\fs24 \
